{"reply":"That scene may have stayed with you because it separates **fixing the visible problem** from **making the mistake visible**. One small correction to your recollection: in Chapter 8, *The Queen’s Croquet-Ground*, Two explains that they planted the wrong rose-tree and are painting it before the Queen arrives: “if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.” When she does arrive, she examines the roses, says “I see!”, and orders the gardeners’ execution—so the concealment does not ultimately keep the mistake from her.

Your note says you fixed the billing mistake and then said nothing because it was “already handled.” The research you mentioned—on hospital nurses—found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety significantly affected willingness to report errors; it also describes fear and perceived interpersonal risk as barriers. That gives us a useful lens, but it cannot establish why you stayed quiet, or whether your particular billing mistake required reporting outside your workplace’s rules ([study](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)).

